# 3. Where to intercept — ranked

### Rank 1 (recommended): three-line in-repo edit + one `<replaceregexp>` — covers 100%, no jar rebuild

**Step 1 — kill 720 of 991 files at the source, purely in-repo.** Delete the two-line pair from each of the three Fortress emitters and replace it with a deterministic provenance line (matching what `ScalaAstGenerator`/`FortressAstGenerator` already do):

- `/home/user/fortress/ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/astgen/TemplateGapClass.java:71-72`
- `/home/user/fortress/ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/astgen/EllipsesNode.java:56-57`
- `/home/user/fortress/ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/astgen/TransformationNode.java:58-59`

Each is exactly:
```java
        writer.startLine(" * @version  Generated automatically by ASTGen at ");
        writer.print(new Date());
```
Replace with e.g. `writer.startLine(" * @version  Generated automatically by ASTGen from Fortress.ast");` and drop the now-unused `import java.util.Date;` (line 18 in `TransformationNode.java` and `EllipsesNode.java`; check `TemplateGapClass.java` for other `Date` uses first). These compile via the `astGenerators` target (`build.xml:420-449`), which already recompiles this exact directory — zero build-plumbing change.

**Step 2 — normalize the remaining 271 (from the jar) with a post-generation ant step.** The `makeAST` target **already does exactly this kind of post-processing**, so the patch slots in with no new machinery (`/home/user/fortress/build.xml:474-498`):

```xml
  <target name="makeAST" unless="nodes.uptodate" depends="checkNodesUptodate"
          description="Automatically generate AST nodes.">
    <echo message="Processing ${generate-sourcefile}" />
    <astgen file="${generate-sourcefile}" />
    <move todir="${nodes}">
      <fileset dir="${astgen.src}">
        <include name="**/*.java"/>
        <exclude name="**/Fortress.ast"/>
      </fileset>
    </move>
    <replace dir="${nodes}"
             token="java.lang.Object"
             value="Object" />
    ...
```

Insert a `<replaceregexp>` immediately after the existing `<replace>` at **line 484-486**, over `${nodes}`, matching `^( \* @version  Generated automatically by ASTGen) at .*$` → `\1 from Fortress.ast`. `${nodes}` resolves to `${src}/${nodesPackage}` (`build.xml:85`), which is the whole 991-file blast radius. Do the same for the 4 Rats! files if you want Family B deterministic too (the `buildparser` macrodef is at `build.xml:1329-1347`; the parser targets are `fortressparser`/`preparser`/`importcollector`/`templateparser`, `build.xml:1349-1372`, all fanned out from `parser`, `build.xml:1402-1409`).

Doing both steps means the tree is stable even if someone regenerates with an unpatched jar.

### Rank 2: patch and rebuild `astgen.jar` — cleanest conceptually, heaviest logistically

`astgen-src.jar` gives you complete, buildable sources (`astgen/astgen/build.xml` plus bundled `lib/plt.jar`, `lib/junit.jar`; manifest `Main-Class: edu.rice.cs.astgen.ASTGen`). Edit `NodeClass.java:95-96`, rebuild, replace both jars. Fixes Family A1 at the true root and needs no build.xml change. Downsides: the vendored jar diverges from upstream Rice (needs a provenance note, cf. `ef45a91ca`'s treatment of BCEL); ASTGen's own build.xml is CVS-era Ant and may need coaxing under JDK 21/Ant 1.10; and **it still leaves 720 files untouched**, so you need Step 1 of Rank 1 regardless. Given that, Rank 2's marginal value over Rank 1 is small.

### Rank 3: add an ASTGen `.ast` option (e.g. `generateTimestamps no;`)

Requires editing `Options.java` (add the field) *and* `ASTGen.java` (add a `_parseGenerateTimestamps()` in the keyword-dispatch chain alongside `_parseAllowNulls()`/`_parseTabSize()`, ~lines 255-320) **and** rebuilding the jar — strictly more work than Rank 2 for the same coverage. Only worth it if you intend to upstream it. The knob would then go in `ProjectFortress/astgen/Fortress.ast`'s options block (~line 25, next to `allowNulls no;`).

### Rank 4 (reject): gitignore `nodes/`

Would remove the churn but breaks the repo's working assumption that generated sources are committed (all 1071 are tracked; `checkNodesUptodate` at `build.xml:451-469` gates on `<available file="${nodes}/AbstractNode.java"/>`, and `CLAUDE.md` documents `touch ProjectFortress/astgen/Fortress.ast && ant compileAll` as the regen recipe). Not recommended.

### Why the churn happens at all (context for whichever fix you pick)

`ant clean` (`build.xml:323-355`) does `<delete dir="${nodes}"/>` (line 326) and deletes the four Rats! outputs plus `Operators.java` and the three `FortressAst.*` files (lines 333-344). That wipes the `<available>`/`<uptodate>` guards in `checkNodesUptodate` (`build.xml:451-469`) and `checkParserUptodate` (`build.xml:1261+`), so `makeAST` and `parser` unconditionally re-run and re-stamp everything. Incremental builds do *not* churn — only `clean` ones, exactly as reported.
